= Memory — Technical =

== Systems and processes ==
Memory taxonomies distinguish systems by content, timescale, conscious accessibility, neural dependence, and task properties.

A useful separation is:

<pre>
encoding → consolidation / retention → retrieval / expression
</pre>

but feedback and reconsolidation make the actual system recurrent rather than linear.

== Episodic and semantic memory ==
Episodic memory supports memory for experienced events in contextual detail.

Semantic memory supports relatively context-independent factual/conceptual knowledge.

The systems interact during learning and retrieval.

== Declarative and nondeclarative memory ==
Declarative memory supports flexible reportable knowledge.

Nondeclarative systems include skills, habits, priming, conditioning, and some perceptual learning.

These categories map imperfectly onto conscious/unconscious distinctions.

== Measurement ==
Recall, recognition, source memory, confidence, response time, savings, priming, neural reinstatement, and behavioral transfer measure different aspects of memory.

